          englishTranslation: "That is indeed distinct from the known, and moreover, It is above the unknown. Thus have we heard from the ancient masters who clearly explained It to us.",
          commentaryNotes: "Śāṅkara Padabhāṣya 1.4: While direct perception and inference cannot teach Brahman, Āgama (tradition of scripture and Guru) can: 'vidita' covers the entire manifested universe (vyākṛta), which is finite and sorrow-laden (to be rejected, heya), while 'avidita' is the unmanifest seed-ignorance (avyākṛta/avidyā) sought as a cause (upādeya). By placing Brahman beyond both 'to-be-rejected' and 'to-be-acquired,' the Śruti proves Brahman is none other than one's own Self—which alone can be neither known nor unknown, and is attainable solely through an unbroken lineage of preceptors."
