          englishTranslation: "The Master says: 'If you think, \"I know Brahman well,\" then surely you know but very little of Its true nature—whether manifest in yourself or among the gods. Therefore, Brahman is still to be investigated by you.' The disciple, after deep contemplation, replies: 'I think It is now comprehended!'",
          commentaryNotes: "Śāṅkara Padabhāṣya 2.1: Lest the disciple mistakenly think 'I know Brahman well as an object,' the teacher shakes his understanding: the Knower cannot be known as an object by the Knower (citing Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.8.11), and even from the same teacher, listeners differ—Virocana mistook the body for Ātman while Indra realized Truth only after four rounds of purification. Though scripture defines Brahman as Consciousness (vijñānam, prajñānam), even those terms indicate Brahman via adjuncts (upādhis) rather than as an objectifiable property; both individual (adhyātma) and cosmic/divine (adhidaiva) forms are limited and therefore 'little' (dabhram). Sitting in solitude with concentrated mind, the disciple ponders scripture, confirms by reason, attains direct Self-realization, and returns to declare: 'I think Brahman is known now.'"
