          englishTranslation: "Of this sacred wisdom, Tapas (inner austerity and focus), Dama (self-restraint of senses), and Karma (dedicated unselfish action) are the firm foundation (feet); the Vedas and their auxiliary limbs are its limbs; and Satya (Truthfulness) is its sacred abode.",
          commentaryNotes: "Śāṅkara Padabhāṣya 4.8: Tapas (concentration of body, senses, and mind), Dama (tranquility), and Karma (ritual/duty) purify the mind across this or previous lives (citing Śvetāśvatara 6.23, Mahābhārata Mokṣadharma, and Gītā 13.7 on humility etc.), serving as the 'feet' on which Brahmavidyā stands. The four Vedas (with the six Vedāṅgas, or viewed figuratively as the remaining limbs like the head) support it, while Satya—straightforwardness without deceit in thought, word, and deed—is highlighted separately as its supreme abode (āyatana), surpassing a thousand Aśvamedha sacrifices."
